\subsection{Sensitivity to IMU Lever-Arm Installation}
\label{sec:imu-lever-arm-study}

The preceding validation places the IMU at the vessel center of gravity (CG).
A real installation may instead be displaced by several decimeters, in which
case rotation produces a translational acceleration at the sensor even when
the CG acceleration is unchanged.  For a body-fixed lever arm $r$ the rigid-
body relation is
%
\begin{equation}
  a_{\mathrm{IMU}}
  = a_{\mathrm{CG}}
  + \dot{\omega}\times r
  + \omega\times(\omega\times r),
  \label{eq:imu-lever-arm}
\end{equation}
%
where $\omega$ and $\dot{\omega}$ are expressed in the body frame.  The first
extra term is tangential acceleration and the second is centripetal
acceleration.  Treating $a_{\mathrm{IMU}}$ as $a_{\mathrm{CG}}$ therefore
injects a deterministic, attitude-correlated model error into the same
accelerometer channel used for both attitude and wave acceleration.

To measure this effect without changing any other part of the estimator, the
standard eight JONSWAP and PM--Stokes records are replayed with the IMU moved
\SIlist{10;20;30}{cm} along each canonical body direction: athwartships,
fore--aft, and vertical.  The record's CG motion, attitude, gyroscope,
magnetometer, stochastic sensor realization, OU adaptation, covariance,
pseudo-measurements, startup logic, and vibration guard are unchanged.  Only
the accelerometer truth is moved from the CG to the sensor location using
\eqref{eq:imu-lever-arm}.

Two matched arms separate installation error from the estimator's remaining
error.  In the \emph{unmodeled} arm, OU--III receives the off-CG specific
force as if it came from the CG.  In the \emph{exact-model} arm, the same
lever vector and the exact angular kinematics are supplied to the measurement
model, so the two rotational terms are removed immediately before fusion.
The latter is intentionally an oracle bound: it measures the error recoverable
from correct lever-arm modeling and does not claim that differentiating a
noisy MEMS gyroscope reproduces exact $\dot{\omega}$ in hardware.

